.TH WHO 1 
.SH NAME
who  \-  who is on the system
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B who
[ who-file ] [
.B "am I"
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Who,
without an argument,
lists the login name, terminal name, and login time
for each current UNIX user.
.PP
Without an argument,
.I who
examines the /etc/utmp
file to obtain its information.
If a file is given, that file is examined.
Typically the given file will be /usr/adm/wtmp,
which contains a record of all the logins since it
was created.
Then
.I who
lists
logins, logouts, and crashes since the creation of
the wtmp file.
Each login is listed with user name,
terminal name (with `/dev/'
suppressed),
and date and time.
When an argument is given, logouts produce a similar line without a user name.
Reboots produce a line with `x' in the place of the device name,
and a fossil time indicative of when the system went down.
.PP
With two arguments,
as in `who am I' (and also `who are you'),
.I who
tells who you are logged in as.
The terminal it reports is the one on the standard input, reduced to a
line name exactly as
.IR login (1)
reduced it when it wrote the record, so the two cannot disagree.
.PP
.B Note:
/usr/adm/wtmp is not kept on this system.
Nothing writes it and nothing reads it, so the paragraph above about
logouts, reboots and fossil times describes a file that has to be made
before it describes anything;
.I who
will read one if it is given one.
.PP
.B Note:
the name and line fields are eight characters and are
.I not
terminated when they are full \- see
.IR utmp (5)
\- so a name of exactly eight characters prints as eight characters and
no more.
Record 0 of /etc/utmp is always blank:
.IR ttyslot (3)
counts the lines of /etc/ttys from 1, and that is the index
.IR login (1)
writes at.
Blank records are suppressed for /etc/utmp and shown when a file is named,
which is what makes a logout visible in a file that keeps them.
.SH FILES
/etc/utmp
.br
/etc/ttys
.SH "SEE ALSO"
getuid(2), utmp(5), ttys(5), login(1), mesg(1), write(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B Note:
v7 printed
.RB ` "who: cannot open utmp" '
on the standard output, naming a file it may not have been given.
The diagnostic names the file that failed and goes to the standard error.
Exit status is 1 in that case and 0 otherwise.
